Q16. SOLID Principle 
Answer: 

1. Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)

A class should have only one reason to change. It should focus on doing exactly one job.
• ❌ Bad: A class that processes an invoice and saves it to a database. If the database schema changes, or the invoice calculation logic changes, this class breaks.
• Good: Separate the calculation logic from the database storage logic.
java
// Invoice class only handles calculation logic
class Invoice {
    public double calculateTotal() { return 100.0; }
}

// InvoiceRepository only handles saving to the database
class InvoiceRepository {
    public void save(Invoice invoice) { /* DB Save Logic */ }
}
Use code with caution.

2. Open/Closed Principle (OCP)

Software entities should be open for extension, but closed for modification. You should be able to add new features without changing existing code.
• ❌ Bad: Using a big if-else or switch block inside a method to handle different payment types. Adding a new payment type means changing the existing class.
• Good: Use an interface. New payment methods just implement the interface without touching old code.
java
interface PaymentProcessor {
    void process(double amount);
}

class CreditCardProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void process(double amount) { /* Process Credit Card */ }
}

class PayPalProcessor implements PaymentProcessor {
    public void process(double amount) { /* Process PayPal */ }
}
Use code with caution.

3. Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)

Subtypes must be substitutable for their base types. A subclass should extend the behavior of a parent class without ruining or breaking its original intent.
• ❌ Bad: Making a Ostrich class extend a Bird class that has a fly() method. An ostrich cannot fly, so calling fly() would throw an exception, breaking the application.
• Good: Break behaviors down so classes only inherit what they actually do.
java
interface Bird { void eat(); }
interface FlyingBird extends Bird { void fly(); }

class Sparrow implements FlyingBird {
    public void eat() {}
    public void fly() {}
}

class Ostrich implements Bird {
    public void eat() {} // No fly() method to break here!
}
Use code with caution.

4. Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)

Clients should not be forced to depend on methods they do not use. It is better to have many small, specific interfaces than one large "God" interface.
• ❌ Bad: A single Worker interface with work() and eatLunch(). If you create a RobotWorker class, it shouldn't be forced to implement an empty eatLunch() method.
• Good: Split the behaviors into lean, single-purpose interfaces.
java
interface Workable { void work(); }
interface Feedable { void eatLunch(); }

class HumanWorker implements Workable, Feedable {
    public void work() {}
    public void eatLunch() {}
}

class RobotWorker implements Workable {
    public void work() {} // Doesn't need to implement eatLunch()
}
Use code with caution.

5. Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. Both should depend on abstractions. Do not hardcode a specific implementation inside another class.
• ❌ Bad: A Car class that explicitly initializes a V8Engine inside its constructor using new V8Engine(). The car is now permanently stuck with a V8 engine and cannot use an electric engine.
• Good: Pass an abstract Engine interface into the Car constructor (Dependency Injection).
java
interface Engine { void start(); }

class V8Engine implements Engine { public void start() {} }
class ElectricEngine implements Engine { public void start() {} }

class Car {
    private Engine engine;

    // The engine is injected from the outside
    public Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}